1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,343
Aarav builds a radio from a kit.

2
00:00:02,643 --> 00:00:06,395
The signal from the aerial
is tiny, about one millivolt.

3
00:00:06,695 --> 00:00:08,824
The speaker needs about two volts.

4
00:00:08,824 --> 00:00:10,953
That is two thousand times bigger.

5
00:00:11,253 --> 00:00:14,226
His amplifier gives a gain of only fifty.

6
00:00:14,226 --> 00:00:15,552
What can he do?

7
00:00:16,052 --> 00:00:19,393
Think of a magnifying glass that
makes things ten times bigger.

8
00:00:19,743 --> 00:00:21,966
Now hold a second one in front of it.

9
00:00:22,316 --> 00:00:25,066
The second glass enlarges
the already enlarged picture.

10
00:00:25,416 --> 00:00:26,610
Ten times ten.

11
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:28,450
One hundred times bigger.

12
00:00:28,450 --> 00:00:29,909
The gains multiply.

13
00:00:30,559 --> 00:00:32,239
Amplifiers work the same way.

14
00:00:32,239 --> 00:00:33,710
We chain them in stages.

15
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:36,777
This is called a multistage amplifier.

16
00:00:37,427 --> 00:00:39,073
But there is a catch.

17
00:00:39,073 --> 00:00:42,087
Each stage has its own
steady bias voltages.

18
00:00:42,437 --> 00:00:45,538
Joining them with a wire would
upset the next stage's bias.

19
00:00:46,188 --> 00:00:49,519
So we join them with the
rubber wall, a capacitor.

20
00:00:50,169 --> 00:00:53,431
Let's build a two stage
amplifier on the breadboard.

21
00:00:53,781 --> 00:00:58,393
Stage one is a CE amplifier,
with voltage divider bias.

22
00:00:58,743 --> 00:01:02,717
Stage two is another CE
amplifier, just like it.

23
00:01:03,067 --> 00:01:06,771
Between them sits the
coupling capacitor, CC.

24
00:01:07,121 --> 00:01:08,472
Its job is simple.

25
00:01:08,472 --> 00:01:09,569
Pass the wave.

26
00:01:09,569 --> 00:01:11,260
Block the steady voltage.

27
00:01:11,610 --> 00:01:13,983
Stage one's collector sits at six volts.

28
00:01:14,333 --> 00:01:17,111
Stage two's base sits at
one point eight volts.

29
00:01:17,111 --> 00:01:18,999
The capacitor keeps them apart.

30
00:01:19,649 --> 00:01:24,122
Each stage uses a resistor as its
load and a capacitor to couple.

31
00:01:24,472 --> 00:01:26,843
That is why it is called RC coupled.

32
00:01:27,493 --> 00:01:29,842
Now feed in a one millivolt wave.

33
00:01:30,192 --> 00:01:32,043
Probe the output of stage one.

34
00:01:32,043 --> 00:01:34,103
Fifty millivolts, and upside down.

35
00:01:34,453 --> 00:01:36,971
Move the probe to the output of stage two.

36
00:01:37,321 --> 00:01:38,221
Two volts!

37
00:01:38,083 --> 00:01:39,573
A gain of two thousand.

38
00:01:39,923 --> 00:01:40,823
And look.

39
00:01:40,593 --> 00:01:42,913
The final wave is the right way up again.

40
00:01:43,563 --> 00:01:46,383
Each CE stage flips the
wave by one eighty degrees.

41
00:01:46,733 --> 00:01:48,859
Two flips make three sixty degrees.

42
00:01:48,859 --> 00:01:50,859
So the output is back in phase.

43
00:01:51,509 --> 00:01:53,996
Now plot the gain at every frequency.

44
00:01:54,346 --> 00:01:58,643
The gain is flat across the middle,
the whole range of sounds we hear.

45
00:01:58,993 --> 00:02:01,655
At low frequencies, the coupling
capacitors block more of the wave.

46
00:02:02,005 --> 00:02:04,618
At high frequencies, hidden
capacitance leaks it away.

47
00:02:05,268 --> 00:02:07,446
Each stage cuts the ends a little.

48
00:02:07,446 --> 00:02:09,100
Two stages cut them more.

49
00:02:09,450 --> 00:02:13,554
So the bandwidth of two stages
is less than that of one stage.

50
00:02:14,204 --> 00:02:18,604
RC coupling is cheap, small, and
gives a flat response for sound.

51
00:02:18,954 --> 00:02:22,392
But it cannot pass very
slow signals, or steady DC.

52
00:02:22,392 --> 00:02:25,021
For those, engineers use direct coupling.

53
00:02:25,671 --> 00:02:26,571
Gains multiply.

54
00:02:26,543 --> 00:02:27,443
Decibels add.

55
00:02:28,137 --> 00:02:30,422
Now the maths, one step at a time.

56
00:02:30,772 --> 00:02:33,656
The overall gain A
equals A one times A two.

57
00:02:34,356 --> 00:02:36,826
Measure A one with stage two connected.

58
00:02:36,826 --> 00:02:38,570
Stage two loads stage one.

59
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:42,478
For n stages, multiply
all n gains together.

60
00:02:43,178 --> 00:02:45,888
In decibels, G equals
twenty times the log of A.

61
00:02:46,588 --> 00:02:48,954
The log of a product is a sum.

62
00:02:48,954 --> 00:02:51,267
So G equals G one plus G two.

63
00:02:51,967 --> 00:02:55,196
Phase: one eighty plus
one eighty is three sixty.

64
00:02:55,196 --> 00:02:56,288
Back in phase.

65
00:02:56,938 --> 00:02:57,976
Worked example.

66
00:02:57,976 --> 00:02:59,450
Find the overall gain.

67
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:01,980
Stage one has a gain of fifty.

68
00:03:01,980 --> 00:03:04,160
Stage two has a gain of forty.

69
00:03:04,510 --> 00:03:06,590
The input is one millivolt.

70
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:09,396
Overall gain is fifty times forty.

71
00:03:09,396 --> 00:03:11,034
That is two thousand.

72
00:03:11,734 --> 00:03:15,006
The output is two thousand
times one millivolt.

73
00:03:15,006 --> 00:03:15,930
Two volts.

74
00:03:16,630 --> 00:03:20,780
In decibels, stage one gives thirty
four, and stage two gives thirty two.

75
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:22,380
Add them.

76
00:03:22,209 --> 00:03:23,437
Sixty six decibels.

77
00:03:23,437 --> 00:03:26,142
And twenty log two
thousand is sixty six too.

78
00:03:26,792 --> 00:03:28,256
Now, the exam trap.

79
00:03:28,606 --> 00:03:30,744
Many students add the stage gains.

80
00:03:30,744 --> 00:03:32,447
Fifty plus forty, ninety.

81
00:03:32,797 --> 00:03:33,697
Wrong.

82
00:03:33,417 --> 00:03:34,921
Plain gains multiply.

83
00:03:34,921 --> 00:03:37,270
Fifty times forty is two thousand.

84
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:39,723
Only gains in decibels add.

85
00:03:39,723 --> 00:03:42,570
And two CE stages give an output in phase.

86
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:44,844
Where is this asked?

87
00:03:44,844 --> 00:03:46,708
In PUC board papers.

88
00:03:47,358 --> 00:03:52,254
Draw and explain a two stage RC
coupled amplifier, with its frequency

89
00:03:52,254 --> 00:03:53,154
response.

90
00:03:53,744 --> 00:03:54,644
Try this.

91
00:03:54,534 --> 00:03:59,061
A three stage RC coupled CE amplifier
has stage gains of twenty, twenty

92
00:03:59,061 --> 00:04:00,785
six and fourteen decibels.

93
00:04:00,785 --> 00:04:03,357
The input is zero point five millivolts.

94
00:04:03,357 --> 00:04:04,542
Find the output.

95
00:04:09,942 --> 00:04:10,842
B.

96
00:04:10,311 --> 00:04:13,644
Zero point five volts, one
eighty degrees out of phase.

97
00:04:13,994 --> 00:04:14,951
Add the decibels.

98
00:04:14,951 --> 00:04:17,599
Twenty plus twenty six plus
fourteen is sixty decibels.

99
00:04:17,949 --> 00:04:20,481
Sixty decibels means a
gain of one thousand.

100
00:04:20,831 --> 00:04:25,018
One thousand times zero point five
millivolts is zero point five volts.

101
00:04:25,368 --> 00:04:28,898
Three flips of one eighty degrees
leave the output upside down.

102
00:04:29,548 --> 00:04:31,094
Screenshot this card.

103
00:04:34,294 --> 00:04:36,872
Next, power amplifiers, class A, B and C.

104
00:04:36,872 --> 00:04:39,198
How does an amplifier push a big speaker?
